# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (shell runtime)
# Bash gotchas surfaced repeatedly during vidya content backfill and in the
# cyrius shell gates: subshell scoping clobbering stateful helpers,
# `(( i++ ))` returning the old value under set -e, `bc` not being
# POSIX-mandatory, and `grep -c` exiting 1 on a legitimate count of 0.
# 6.7.10: +1 entry — the_shell_reports_its_own_process_not_your_command_v6710 (a subshell's crash
# message, `| head &&`, per-worker binary names). 5 entries.
# 6.7.12: +5 entries — the toolchain-workflow gotchas a cyrius user meets at the shell:
# cyrius_watch_output_is_unsigned_on_apple_silicon_v6712, a_windows_checkout_rewrites_a_committed_lib_to_crlf_v6712,
# bootstrap_sh_builds_no_cycc_v6712, a_formatters_rewrite_and_its_check_accept_different_sets_v6712,
# pin_bump_order_with_a_full_vendored_lib_v6712. 10 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.12).
# 6.7.13: +1 entry — a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713 (D39). Updated: pin_bump_order_with_a_full_vendored_
# lib_v6712 (D41, D42, TL-10). 11 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.13).
# 6.7.14: no new entry. Updated: cyrius_watch_output_is_unsigned_on_apple_silicon_v6712 (the watcher runs `cyrius build`
# since D60, and `cat | cycc` is not a project build), a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713 (the signal is
# forwarded to the CLI's own git, TL-6; the unbounded wait filed). 11 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.14).
# 6.7.15 (2026-10-19): +1 entry — a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715 (the std-fd
# class: LF-6, LF-9). Updated: a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713 (the forwarded git gets 2 s, then SIGKILL,
# D49; a killed scratch resolve unlinks only its own temps, IT-12). 12 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.15).
# 6.7.16 (2026-10-21): +2 entries — posix_mode_bash_kill_0_of_a_non_number_answers_0_v6716 (LF-4),
# what_cyrius_lint_and_cyrius_lsp_accept_v6716 (the language server's compile unit and `[lsp] skip`, TL-13, TL-15;
# cycc_cx `--syntax-only`, TL-10; the lint verdicts the lanes moved). Updated: a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_
# that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715 (cbt/, programs/ and sigil 3.13.13 take the guard and the lift, LF-6; the parent's
# own mute and the unused pipe end; `env` hides the bug on pi; `ssh -GF/dev/null`; the binfmt note, PL-11). 14 entries
# (DERIVED at 6.7.16).

[[entries]]
name = "bash_subshell_clobbers_stateful_helpers"
description = "`$(fn)` runs the function in a subshell — global mutations do NOT propagate to the caller; use a side-effect global instead"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP (4× repeat across v2.3.3 → v2.3.4 backfill):

A stateful helper (e.g. a PRNG) updates a global, then the caller
captures the return value via `$(...)`. The mutation is silently
lost — `$(...)` runs the body in a forked subshell, and the
subshell's environment changes never reach the parent.

    _rng_state=42

    rng_next() {
        _rng_state=$(( _rng_state * 6364136223846793005 + 1442695040888963407 ))
        echo $(( _rng_state & 0xFFFFFFFF ))
    }

    # WRONG — every call returns the same number:
    for i in 1 2 3; do
        x=$(rng_next)        # subshell: _rng_state mutates, then dies
        echo "x=$x"
    done

The `for` loop body runs in the parent shell, so the parent's
`_rng_state` is the only one that matters — and it never updates.
First call: subshell forks, computes new state, echoes 32-bit
slice, exits, parent's `_rng_state` is unchanged. Second call:
subshell forks again from the same parent state. All three `x`
values match.

CORRECT SHAPE — side-effect global, no `$(...)`:

    _rng_state=42
    RNG_OUT=0

    rng_next() {
        _rng_state=$(( _rng_state * 6364136223846793005 + 1442695040888963407 ))
        RNG_OUT=$(( _rng_state & 0xFFFFFFFF ))
    }

    for i in 1 2 3; do
        rng_next             # runs in parent shell — both globals update
        echo "x=$RNG_OUT"
    done

The function call (without `$(...)`) executes in the current
shell. Both the state and the output land in the parent.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  game_ai_decisions/shell.sh — PCG PRNG via `$(rng_next)`.
          Every "random" stat came back identical; the AI dispatch
          test failed deterministically with the wrong action.
          1st surfacing.
  v2.3.3  maze_generation/shell.sh — same pattern, masked the
          PCG state-machine entirely. 2nd surfacing.
  v2.3.4  btree_indexing/shell.sh — `node_new_leaf` mutated a
          counter, returned the new id via stdout. Subshell
          captured the id but the parent never saw the bump,
          so every "new" leaf got id=1. 3rd surfacing.
  v2.3.4  write_ahead_logging/shell.sh — full WAL state (offsets,
          last-applied-lsn, in-memory page cache) was held in
          globals; every `wal_append=$(append ...)` clobbered
          them. 4th surfacing — broke replay tests in 6
          different scenarios before the pattern was noticed.

WHY IT REPEATS: The `$(fn)` form looks like a clean
return-value capture, the way a Rust or Python function returns.
Bash makes the same syntax mean "fork a subshell, read its
stdout." The two are visually identical; the semantics are
opposite.

GENERAL RULE for bash ports of stateful algorithms in vidya:
- If the function only reads globals and produces output:
  `result=$(fn args)` is fine.
- If the function MUTATES any global: never use `$(...)`. Use
  a `_OUT` companion global, call the function bare, read the
  global. Document it: `# rng_next: writes _rng_state, sets RNG_OUT`.
- If you can't tell at a glance: assume it mutates and use the
  side-effect form.

This pattern is documented in the Bash man page under "Command
Substitution" — but only obliquely: "the command is executed in
a subshell environment." No example, no warning. Easy to miss.

[[entries]]
name = "bash_pre_increment_set_e_zero_exit"
description = "`(( i++ ))` evaluates to the OLD value of i; when i=0 that's exit code 0 → set -e aborts the script. The general class: under `set -e` the status of a command you meant to INSPECT is consumed before you can read it — `cmd; rc=$?` is dead code for a failing cmd (sigil 3.13.7's fuzz loop, cyrius's release gate); write `rc=0; cmd || rc=$?`"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    set -euo pipefail
    i=0
    while [[ $i -lt 10 ]]; do
        # ... do work ...
        (( i++ ))                # script exits here on first iteration
    done
    echo "done"                  # never printed

`(( expr ))` exits with status 0 if the expression is non-zero,
status 1 if it's zero. The C-style `i++` is post-increment: it
evaluates to the OLD value, then bumps. When `i=0`, the
expression evaluates to 0, the arithmetic command exits 1, and
`set -e` treats it as a failure and aborts.

This bites every counter that starts at 0. The first iteration
hits the wall; the rest never run. The visible symptom is a
truncated loop, no error message — `set -e` aborts silently
unless `set -E` + a trap is also set.

CORRECT SHAPES — three options, all equivalent:

    i=$((i + 1))            # plain assignment, always exit 0
    : $(( i++ ))             # leading `:` is a no-op, swallows the
                              # arithmetic command's exit status
    (( ++i ))                # PRE-increment: evaluates to NEW value,
                              # which is non-zero (assuming i was >= 0)

Pick `i=$((i+1))`. It's unambiguous, doesn't depend on knowing
the post/pre-increment distinction, and matches the explicit
arithmetic form used elsewhere.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.4  sql_parsing/shell.sh — counter loop over SQL token
          stream. First iteration aborted; the parse table built
          to one entry then the script silently terminated.

WHY IT'S NOT IN MOST BASH STYLE GUIDES: the interaction is
between two features (`set -e` + `(( ))` exit semantics) that
each have their own documented behaviour, but the combination
isn't called out together. ShellCheck (SC2219) flags
`(( i++ ))` only in the narrow case of a standalone arithmetic
command — not in loops, not when `set -e` is active. In
practice, the only reliable defence is "use `i=$((i+1))` for
counter increments, period."

GENERAL RULE for bash ports in vidya: **never use `(( i++ ))`
or `(( i-- ))` as a standalone statement**. Use
`i=$((i+1))` / `i=$((i-1))`. It's two extra characters and
it's safe under any `set` flags.

THE SAME CLASS, WIDER: under `set -e`, a status you meant to
INSPECT is consumed by `set -e` first.

    timeout 5 "$bin" >/dev/null 2>&1
    rc=$?                                  # never reached when rc != 0
    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || [ "$rc" -eq 124 ]; then ...   # 124 accepted

sigil's scripts/check.sh ran its fuzz harnesses in exactly this
shape until 3.13.7 (found by the cyrius 6.6.14 sigil lane):
fuzz_ed25519 (~11 s) hit the 5 s timeout and the audit exited 124
with NO summary on every run — after its tests and benches had
passed, so it read as "the audit is slow", not "the audit is
broken". cyrius's release gate graded check.sh the same way
(field_notes/compiler/methodology.cyml, `grading_a_subprocess_...`).
The shape that works:

    rc=0
    timeout 5 "$bin" >/dev/null 2>&1 || rc=$?

RULE: under `set -e`, every `cmd; rc=$?` is dead code for a
failing `cmd`. Write `rc=0; cmd || rc=$?`.

[[entries]]
name = "grep_dash_c_prints_zero_and_exits_one"
description = "`grep -c` on no match PRINTS `0` and EXITS 1 — the two are not alternatives. So the reflex `$(grep -c … || echo 0)` yields the two-line string \"0\\n0\" and every comparison against it fails. Use `|| true`, not `|| echo 0`."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    n=$(grep -c "pattern" file || echo 0)
    if [ "$n" -eq 0 ]; then ...     # integer expression expected

`grep -c` is a COUNTER, not a matcher: on no match it still does its job
and prints `0`. But it ALSO reports "nothing matched" through its exit
status, which is 1. Those are two independent channels, and the `|| echo
0` reflex assumes they are alternatives — that a failing grep printed
nothing. It printed `0`, then `echo` printed `0` again, and the capture
is the two-line string `0\n0`.

MEASURED (bash, GNU grep, 2026-08-07):

    $ grep -c zzz f.txt ; echo "exit=$?"
    0
    exit=1

    a=$(grep -c zzz f.txt || true)      # a = "0"          ← CORRECT
    b=$(grep -c zzz f.txt) || true      # b = "0"          ← also correct
    c=$(grep -c zzz f.txt || echo 0)    # c = "0\n0"       ← THE BUG
    d=$(grep zzz f.txt | wc -l)         # d = "0"          ← no -c, no exit-1

CORRECT SHAPES — pick one:

  1. `n=$(grep -c "pat" file || true)` — swallow the status, keep the
     count. Shortest and says what it means.
  2. `n=$(grep -c "pat" file) || true` — same result; the `|| true`
     applies to the assignment rather than inside the substitution.
  3. `n=$(grep "pat" file | wc -l)` — sidesteps it entirely, since
     `wc` always exits 0. (Under `set -o pipefail` grep's 1 still
     propagates, so this one needs its own `|| true` there.)

Never `|| echo 0`. It is only correct for a command that prints
NOTHING when it fails, and `grep -c` is precisely not that.

WHY YOU NEED THE `|| true` AT ALL: under `set -e`, `n=$(failing_cmd)`
takes the substitution's exit status as the assignment's, so the script
aborts on a legitimate count of zero — which for a gate is the *normal*
case ("assert this pattern is absent"). This is the same class as
`(( i++ ))` above: a command whose exit status encodes data, meeting a
`set -e` that reads exit status as failure.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v6.5.10  a cyrius release gate counted occurrences in generated
           output with `$(grep -c … || echo 0)`. Every comparison
           against the count failed with "integer expression expected",
           and the two-line value is invisible in an unquoted echo.
           Cost one debug cycle; noted in the gate.

GENERAL RULE for cyrius shell gates: the gates run under
`bash -eo pipefail`, so ANY bookkeeping command whose exit status carries
information — `grep`, `diff`, `cmp`, `test` — needs an explicit `|| true`
(or an `if`) at the capture site. See also the CI shell-loop rule in
CLAUDE.md: `var=$(failing_cmd)` trips `set -e` before the bookkeeping
runs, which is how SKIP/XFAIL accounting goes silently missing.

[[entries]]
name = "bash_bc_not_posix_mandatory"
description = "`bc` isn't part of POSIX-mandatory userland; default Arch + minimal Alpine omit it. Use `awk` for floating-point math (sqrt/log/exp)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    # Compute sqrt of x in shell
    val=$(echo "scale=10; sqrt($x)" | bc -l)

`bc` is a POSIX **utility**, but POSIX-2017 doesn't require
distributions to ship it by default. Default Arch installs
omit it (`pacman -S bc` is needed). Alpine's `BusyBox bc` is a
limited subset. NixOS minimal builds don't include it.

Result: a shell script that uses `bc` works on Ubuntu, fails
silently or with "command not found" on Arch / Alpine / minimal
NixOS / many container base images. CI may pass while local
runs on a fresh Arch box don't.

CORRECT SHAPE — `awk` for floating-point math. POSIX requires
`awk` (it's been in `/usr/bin/awk` on every Linux/BSD since the
1980s) and the math built-ins (`sqrt`, `log`, `exp`, `sin`,
`cos`, `atan2`, `**` for power) cover everything `bc -l` does
short of arbitrary precision.

    sqrt_helper() {
        awk -v x="$1" 'BEGIN { printf "%.10f\n", sqrt(x) }'
    }

    log_helper() {
        awk -v x="$1" 'BEGIN { printf "%.10f\n", log(x) }'
    }

    exp_helper() {
        awk -v x="$1" 'BEGIN { printf "%.10f\n", exp(x) }'
    }

    pow_helper() {
        awk -v b="$1" -v e="$2" 'BEGIN { printf "%.10f\n", b**e }'
    }

`-v name=value` plumbs the shell value into awk safely (no
shell-injection risk via the script string). `BEGIN` runs the
block before any input is read, so awk doesn't wait on stdin.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.2  quantum_computing/shell.sh — three helpers used
          `bc -l` for state-vector amplitude math (sqrt for
          normalisation, log/exp for entropy bounds, ** for
          gate matrix powers). Default-Arch `validate-content.sh`
          run failed on all three; Ubuntu CI passed. Replaced
          with awk, identical numerical output to ~1e-15.

GENERAL RULE for shell ports in vidya: **assume only the POSIX
core is present**. That means `awk`, `sed`, `grep`, `cut`,
`tr`, `sort`, `uniq`, `head`, `tail`, `wc`, `printf`, `read`,
`test` / `[`, `expr` (for integer arithmetic — but bash's
`$(( ))` is preferred), and the bash arithmetic forms. No
`bc`, no `dc`, no GNU-only extensions to coreutils, no
`gawk`-specific features.

For arbitrary-precision integer math: bash's `$(( ))` works
to 64 bits, beyond that use `python3 -c "..."` (POSIX-y enough
on Linux distros that ship a python3) or stay in 64 bits.

For arbitrary-precision floating-point: there's no portable
answer. If the algorithm needs > 53-bit floats, port to a
language other than shell.

[[entries]]
name = "the_shell_reports_its_own_process_not_your_command_v6710"
description = "Met in 6.7.10's coverage gates: `(cmd) 2>/dev/null` does not silence a crashing child's \"Segmentation fault\" (bash execs a subshell's last command; `(cmd; exit $?) 2>/dev/null` does); `cmp … | head && cp` grades HEAD's status, so the copy runs regardless; parallel corpus workers need a unique binary name per file and per leg (a lingering forked child holds the binary: \"Text file busy\")."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE CRASH MESSAGE COMES FROM THE SHELL THAT WAITED. A shell-run test that crashes prints the
job message ("Segmentation fault") into the gate log, and the redirect on a subshell does not
reach it: bash execs the LAST command of `( … )` directly, so the shell that waits — and
prints — is the outer one.

    (./crasher) 2>/dev/null            # still prints "Segmentation fault"
    (./crasher; exit $?) 2>/dev/null   # a real shell in between: exit status 139, nothing printed

A PIPELINE'S STATUS IS ITS LAST COMMAND'S. `cmp a b | head && cp a b` grades `head`, which
succeeds, so the copy runs whether the files matched or not (it emptied a file in the P5-B lane;
restored from a scratch copy). Test the command itself (`cmp -s a b && …`), or set `pipefail`
and know that it then reports the pipeline's LAST nonzero status.

PARALLEL WORKERS NEED A UNIQUE BINARY NAME PER FILE AND PER LEG. A test's lingering forked child
keeps its binary open, so a worker that reuses the name reads "Text file busy" — a failure that
moves between runs. Name each output after the file AND the leg (and the worker), never a fixed
scratch name.

Related: bash_pre_increment_set_e_zero_exit (a status you meant to INSPECT under `set -e`),
grep_dash_c_prints_zero_and_exits_one. CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (P5-B).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_watch_output_is_unsigned_on_apple_silicon_v6712"
description = "`cat f.cyr | cycc` is not a project build: it resolves `include` against the current directory, applies no manifest, and on Apple Silicon writes an unsigned binary AMFI kills at exec (137). The macOS tarballs' `cyrius-watch.sh` built that way through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 (D60) it runs `cyrius build`, and its output launches. A script that compiles a user project calls `cyrius build`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.14", "macos", "apple silicon", "codesign", "AMFI", "cyrius-watch.sh", "cyrius build", "include", "#@incdir", "CYRIUS_BIN", "D60"]
---
- `cat f.cyr | cycc > out` is NOT a project build. It resolves `include "x.cyr"` against the current directory, not
  f.cyr's; it applies no manifest ([deps], prepended includes); and on Apple Silicon it writes an UNSIGNED binary that
  AMFI kills at exec (exit 137; the same binary exits 42 after `codesign -s -`). `cyrius build` writes the `#@incdir`
  marker (so `include` resolves relative to the entry file), prepends [deps], ad-hoc signs on macOS, shows the
  compiler's diagnostic and keeps the previous output when a build fails. A script that compiles a user project must
  call `cyrius build`; raw `cat | cycc` is for the compiler's own self-host.
- `cyrius-watch.sh` (in every POSIX tarball — the macOS ones since 6.7.12, the aarch64-linux one since 6.7.14) compiled
  with raw `cat | cycc` through 6.7.13, so its output was killed on Apple Silicon and an entry-relative `include` failed
  under it on EVERY OS. Since 6.7.14 (D60, PL-8) each build is `cyrius build "$SRC" "$OUT"`: the output launches. The
  CLI it runs is `CYRIUS_BIN`, else `cyrius` on PATH, else `$CYRIUS_HOME/bin/cyrius`; `CYRIUS_CC` is ignored now (the
  CLI's own resolution picks the compiler). A project it used to build without the manifest's prepends now builds with
  them. Run it as `cyrius-watch.sh [src] [out]` — there is still no `cyrius watch` verb.
language/tooling.cyml helper_scripts. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] DW-6, [6.7.14] PL-8.

[[entries]]
name = "a_windows_checkout_rewrites_a_committed_lib_to_crlf_v6712"
description = "cyrius.lock hashes the exact bytes `cyrius deps` wrote; Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites a committed lib/ to CRLF on checkout, so `deps --verify` and `--locked` fail on every file. Since 6.7.12 the failure names it. The cure is `lib/** -text` in `.gitattributes`, committed, then check lib/ out again — never re-lock the converted bytes."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "windows", "git", "autocrlf", "CRLF", "gitattributes", "cyrius.lock", "deps --verify", "--locked"]
---
cyrius.lock hashes the EXACT bytes `cyrius deps` wrote. Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites a
committed lib/ to CRLF on checkout, so `cyrius deps --verify` and `--locked` fail on every file. Since 6.7.12 the
failure names the cause (`… CRLF checkout: add `lib/** -text` to .gitattributes`).

The cure: `lib/** -text` in `.gitattributes`, committed, then check lib/ out again. NEVER re-lock the converted bytes —
`cyrius deps` would, and the lock then churns between Windows and Linux checkouts. A CRLF checkout still FAILS
verification by design: exact bytes are the proof. `cyrius init` writes the line and `cyrius port` creates or appends it
(D10). language/tooling.cyml (the cyrius.lock paragraph). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] TL-27.

[[entries]]
name = "bootstrap_sh_builds_no_cycc_v6712"
description = "`bootstrap/bootstrap.sh` assembles cybs from the seed and builds asm — never a cycc. `scripts/seed-derive-cycc.sh` PROVES the tracked build/cycc descends from the seed and itself needs build/cycc to exist. Neither makes a missing build/cycc: restore it with `git checkout build/cycc`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "bootstrap", "seed", "cybs", "build/cycc", "seed-derive", "cyriusly setup"]
---
- `bootstrap/bootstrap.sh` assembles cybs from the 29 KB seed and builds asm — it never produces a cycc.
- `scripts/seed-derive-cycc.sh` PROVES that the tracked build/cycc descends from the seed (seed → cybs → gen1 → gen2 ==
  build/cycc), and itself needs build/cycc to exist (`ERROR: build/cycc missing`).
- Neither makes a missing build/cycc. Restore it with `git checkout build/cycc` (the tracked compiler is what CI and the
  installer bootstrap from). `cyriusly setup` refuses by name without it since 6.7.12 — it used to run 15 compiles
  against the missing compiler and leave 15 zero-byte tools.
language/tooling.cyml cyriusly_native_port_v51110. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] TL-4.

[[entries]]
name = "a_formatters_rewrite_and_its_check_accept_different_sets_v6712"
description = "`cyrfmt <file>` writes the CANONICAL form (a 2-space continuation step); `cyrfmt --check` accepts 2 or 4. Anything that judges 'formatted' must ask the check, never byte-compare the rewrite — `cyrius audit` did until 6.7.12 and flagged files `--check` passed (D26)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "cyrfmt", "fmt", "--check", "audit", "continuation indent", "D26"]
---
`cyrfmt <file>` writes the CANONICAL form (a 2-space continuation step per open paren level); `cyrfmt --check` accepts 2
OR 4 (the 2026-08-18 formatter contract; deeper is rejected). So "is it formatted?" has two answers depending on whom you
ask. Anything that judges "formatted" must ask the CHECK, never byte-compare the rewrite with the file — `cyrius audit`
did until 6.7.12 and flagged files `--check` passed (D26, TL-20). To REQUIRE the canonical form exactly, compare
`cyrfmt <file>` with the file yourself — and know that is a stricter rule than the contract. `cyrius fmt` rewrites an
accepted 4 as 2. language/tooling.cyml audit_test_check_pipeline (LAYER 2). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] TL-20.

[[entries]]
name = "pin_bump_order_with_a_full_vendored_lib_v6712"
description = "In a project that vendors the whole snapshot, run `cyrius lib sync --full` FIRST after moving `[package].cyrius`; since 6.7.12 `cyrius deps` / `cyrius build` refuse to re-lock until then (rc 1, the stale files named, the lock unchanged — D19), and since 6.7.13 a refused run leaves lib/ untouched too (D41) and every other lock writer refuses the same way (D42)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.13", "pin bump", "lib sync", "--full", "cyrius.lock", "deps", "D19", "D41", "D42", "--relock"]
---
    # edit [package] cyrius = "6.7.12"
    cyrius lib sync --full        # FIRST: re-vendors the whole snapshot and re-locks what it wrote
    cyrius deps --verify          # passes

In a project that vendors the WHOLE snapshot, `cyrius lib sync --full` goes first after moving `[package].cyrius`.
Since 6.7.12 `cyrius deps` / `cyrius build` at the moved pin REFUSE to re-lock while lib/ holds an out-of-closure file
the new snapshot ships with other bytes (rc 1, each stale file named, cyrius.lock unchanged — D19); `--relock` does not
bypass it. Under 6.7.12 the closure files were already at the new pin after the refusal, so `--verify` failed on them
until the remedy ran. Since 6.7.13: a refused `deps` / `build` leaves lib/ AND the lock untouched (a silent scratch
resolve learns the closure first — D41); `deps --locked` names the same remedy (TL-10); and a bare `deps --lock` and a
scoped `lib sync` / `update` refuse too (D42). So the recipe is simply `cyrius lib sync --full` first, and every verb
that would stamp the pin points there. language/tooling.cyml lib_sync_relock_v6617. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] TL-12, TL-12r;
[6.7.13] TL-10, TL-11, TL-12.

[[entries]]
name = "a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713"
description = "A `cyrius` stopped by SIGTERM / SIGINT / SIGHUP cleans up its children, scratch and temp dir, then DIES of the signal (6.7.13, D39). A shell's `$?` reads 128+N on every version, but a parent reading the wait status saw WIFEXITED 128+N on 6.7.12 alone — so bash's SIGINT loop-stop did not fire for `cyrius` there. With `exec_capture_status`, test st[1] (the signal bit), not the code. Since 6.7.14 it first forwards the signal to its own git and waits for it — since 6.7.15 for at most 2 s, then SIGKILL (D49), so a `git` wrapper's cleanup must fit in 2 s."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "git", "signals", "SIGINT", "SIGTERM", "SIGHUP", "SIGKILL", "WIFSIGNALED", "WIFEXITED", "exec_capture_status", "D39", "D49", "cleanup"]
---
    for f in *.cyr; do cyrius build "$f" "bin/${f%.cyr}" || exit 1; done   # Ctrl-C stops the loop again (6.7.13)

    var st: i64[2];
    exec_capture_status(args, buf, n, &st);    # args: a vec of cstrs
    if (st[1] == 1) { … }          # killed by a signal — st[1], not `st[0] == 130`

A `cyrius` run stopped by SIGTERM / SIGINT / SIGHUP stops its children and removes its scratch and temp dir, then
re-raises the signal and dies of it. A shell's `$?` reads 128+N either way (143 / 130 / 129) — the difference is the
WAIT STATUS: WIFSIGNALED on 6.7.11 and on 6.7.13 onward, but WIFEXITED 128+N on 6.7.12 alone, which is why a Ctrl-C'd
`cyrius` did not stop a non-interactive shell loop there (bash stops its loop only on a child that DIED of SIGINT). A
cyrius parent reading the status should test `exec_capture_status`'s st[1] (1 = killed by a signal), not the code
alone. An inherited SIG_IGN (nohup, a `&` job's SIGINT) is kept. Windows catches nothing.
Since 6.7.14 a killed `cyrius deps` / `build` FORWARDS the signal to its own git (the cached checkout's verify, a clone)
and waits for it before it dies, so git's own cleanup runs — a killed clone removes its partial cache directory — and the
git captures and the scratch resolve of `deps --locked` / the pin-move dry pass are removed too (TL-6, TL-7). In 6.7.14
the forwarded wait had no bound: a `git` first on PATH (a wrapper) that traps or ignores the signal held the CLI for as
long as it ran, and a second Ctrl-C was held too. Since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D49, IT-11) git gets 2 s after the forwarded
signal, then SIGKILL: a killed `cyrius deps` (or a build that resolves) exits within about 2 s whatever git does. A
wrapper's own cleanup must fit in those 2 s; real git exits on the signal, runs its cleanup and is unaffected (a killed
clone still removes its partial directory). And a killed scratch resolve unlinks only the temp files it CREATED — a file
another process put at a temp name the copy skipped stays (IT-12). `$?` and the signal death (D39) are unchanged.
Windows has no POSIX signals: unchanged. language/tooling.cyml cyrius_cli (A KILLED RUN). CHANGELOG [6.7.13] TL-18
(D39), [6.7.14] TL-6, TL-7, [6.7.15] IT-11, IT-12.

[[entries]]
name = "a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715"
description = "CLASS (6.7.15). A process started with fd 0, 1 or 2 closed (`<&-`, `>&-`, a supervisor, a daemon) hands that NUMBER to its next open or pipe, so a child-redirect sequence written for the normal case turns on itself: `dup2(fd, N); close(fd)` closes the redirect when fd already IS N, and a parent-opened output fd sitting at 0 is overwritten by the child's own stdin redirect. Close a redirect's source only when fd != N, and lift every parent-opened fd above 2 (`fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD, 3)`) before any redirect. lib/process.cyr, lib/regression.cyr and lib/pam.cyr do since 6.7.15; the `cyrius` CLI, the check driver, programs/ (cyrius-lsp included) and sigil 3.13.13's run-capture verbs since 6.7.16 — `cyrius build x.cyr out 0<&-` shipped an empty program and said OK through 6.7.15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "fd", "stdin", "stdout", "stderr", "dup2", "close", "fork", "exec", "redirect", "capture", "pipe", "process.cyr", "regression.cyr", "pam.cyr", "cbt", "programs", "sigil", "F_DUPFD", "/dev/fd", "env", "ssh -G", "qemu", "binfmt"]
---
    ./prog <&- >&-          # prog starts with fds 0 and 1 closed: its first pipe() answers (0, 1)

THE MECHANISM. open, pipe and dup answer the LOWEST free fd. A process started with a std fd closed — by a shell's `<&-`
/ `>&-`, a supervisor, a daemon that closed its std fds — hands that number to its next open or pipe: with fds 0 and 1
closed `pipe()` answers (0, 1); with fd 2 closed `open("/dev/null")` answers 2. A child-redirect sequence written for
the normal case then turns on itself, four ways:
  1. `dup2(fd, N); close(fd)` CLOSES THE REDIRECT WHEN fd ALREADY IS N (LF-6). `dup2(1, 1)` is a no-op, so the close
     after it closes the pipe's only write end: the exec'd program starts with stdout closed and the capture reads 0
     bytes. With fd 2 closed, `dup2(2, 2); close(2)` leaves the program with fd 2 closed — its first open lands on 2,
     and its diagnostics go into that file.
  2. A PARENT-OPENED fd CAN BE 0, 1 OR 2, AND THE CHILD'S OWN REDIRECT OVERWRITES IT (LF-9). With fd 0 closed the output
     file opens at 0; the child's stdin redirect (`dup2(devnull, 0)`, or the pipe's read end) replaces it before the
     output redirect reads it, so stdout goes to /dev/null or the pipe and the capture is empty. Reordering the
     redirects is not enough once the child carries two parent fds: with fds 0 and 1 closed the output is at 0 and the
     pipe at 1, so stdin-first overwrites the output and out-first overwrites the pipe.
  3. THE PARENT'S OWN MUTE (6.7.16, LF-6). `dup2(nul, 1); dup2(nul, 2); close(nul)` in a parent started with fd 1 or 2
     closed closes that stream again, because `nul` opened AT it; a child spawned while muted inherits it closed, and
     its first file becomes its stdout or stderr. Close only when `nul` is neither 1 nor 2 (cbt's `_cbt_mute_set`).
  4. THE UNUSED PIPE END (6.7.16, LF-6). A pipe child that redirects first and closes the unused end after can close its
     own redirect: with fd 1 closed the pipe's read end is 1, and the tar child's `close(rfd)` after `dup2(wfd, 1)`
     closed tar's stdout. Close the unused end BEFORE redirecting (programs/checks' `_tar_pipe_ssh`).

THE RULE. In the child, before redirecting any std fd, move every parent-opened fd that is 2 or below above 2 —
`fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD, 3)`, then close the old number (if F_DUPFD fails, carry on with the fd unchanged) — and close a
redirect's source only when `fd != N` (when one fd feeds two targets, only when it is neither). With every std fd open,
the normal case, both are no-ops and the child setup is identical.

WHERE IT STANDS. Since 6.7.15 lib/process.cyr's capture and env verbs (`_proc_redirect`), lib/regression.cyr's
redirecting verbs (`_regression_redirect`; `_regression_lift_fd` for the caller-file verbs
`regression_exec_in_dir3[_env]`, `regression_pipe_to_bin_capture` and `regression_ssh_remote_exec_capture`) and
lib/pam.cyr give the child's real output whichever std fds the caller closed, and the exec'd child starts with fds 0, 1
and 2 open (stdin on /dev/null or the pipe). Since 6.7.16 the rest of the tree takes the guard and the lift (LF-6):
every child redirect in cbt/ (the `cyrius` CLI: `_cbt_redirect` / `_cbt_lift_fd`) and programs/ (the check driver, the
tools; cyrius-lsp through the tools lane's `_lsp_redirect`), and pulsar's redirect order. Through 6.7.15, with a std fd
closed, `cyrius build --target=js x.ts out.js 1>&-` failed "could not write the output (0 of 29 bytes): errno 9",
`cyrius build h.cyr hb 0<&-` said OK and shipped an EMPTY program (cycc read an empty stdin), `cyrius deps 0<&-` called
a clean cache "tampered", and the check driver's rows read "compile error". `cli_child_redirect_closed_std_fds.sh`'s
census keeps every unguarded `sys_dup2(X, N); sys_close(X)` pair out of cbt/ and programs/. The folded sigil's
`agnosys_run_capture[_timeout]` / `agnosys_run_checked[_timeout]` skip a redirect whose fd already is the target since
sigil 3.13.13 (folded in 6.7.16): a caller with a std fd closed gets its capture (it was Err).

TESTING IT. Run each row in a FORKED copy of the test that closes the fds first and answers through its exit code, and
spawn the test itself as a probe child that reports which of fds 0, 1 and 2 it started with — every mask of the three
(`tests/tcyr/crossos/process_capture_closed_std_fds.tcyr`, `regression_capture_stdin_closed.tcyr`). Probe an fd with
`/proc/self/fd/N` on Linux and `/dev/fd/N` on macOS, which has no `/proc`. Three more traps (6.7.16):
  - `env` HIDES THE BUG ON pi. On pi (Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-1020-raspi) a coreutils program started with a std fd closed
    has it reopened on /dev/null (measured with `env` and `readlink`; cyrius-built binaries and dash do not), so a
    closed-fd row that launches through `env HOME=… cyrius … 0<&-` reads GREEN on the bug there — green on the OLD CLI
    on pi, red without `env`. Let the shell exec the program, and prove the closure with a probe that exits with a bit
    per fd it started without (`cli_child_redirect_closed_std_fds.sh`'s premise row).
  - DRIVING AN ssh VERB WITH NO HOST. `ssh -GF/dev/null <name>` prints the resolved configuration to stdout and exits 0
    without connecting; `-F /dev/null` reads neither `~/.ssh/config` nor `/etc/ssh/ssh_config`, so no `Match exec` runs
    (the bundled form equals `-G -F /dev/null` on OpenSSH 9.0 – 10.6). That is how `regression_out_path_truncated.tcyr`
    and `regression_capture_stdin_closed.tcyr` exercise `regression_ssh_remote_exec_capture`.
  - A self-spawning test fails under a bare `qemu-aarch64` with no binfmt registration (the exec of argv(0) answers
    127). Run it on aarch64 hardware, or register qemu with binfmt_misc as `docs/development/dev-tools-linux.md` says
    since 6.7.16 (`qemu-user-binfmt`; check `ls /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/qemu-aarch64`; `scripts/dev/xb.sh` warns when
    it is absent — PL-11; platform_abi.cyml wine_and_qemu_user_are_not_the_platforms_they_emulate_v6716).
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] LF-6, LF-9; [6.7.16] LF-6, W0-4 (sigil 3.13.13), PL-11.

[[entries]]
name = "posix_mode_bash_kill_0_of_a_non_number_answers_0_v6716"
description = "`kill -0 123x` exits 0 under bash run as `/bin/sh` (posix mode) and non-zero under dash, so a liveness check on an unvalidated pid reads 'alive' under one shell and 'dead' under the other. Validate the pid's shape first, as one `case` on the whole string — a digits-only test after `kill -0` is masked by bash."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "bash", "dash", "posix mode", "/bin/sh", "kill", "pid", "liveness", "case", "check.sh", "reaper"]
---
    kill -0 123x; echo $?        # bash as /bin/sh (posix mode): 0      dash: non-zero
    case "$_r" in ''|*[!0-9.]*|.*|*.|*.*.*) continue ;; esac     # check.sh's shape check, BEFORE kill -0

THE TRAP. Posix-mode bash answers `kill -0 <non-number>` with 0; dash refuses it. A liveness check on unvalidated input
— a pid parsed out of a file name, a lock file, a log — therefore reads "alive" under one shell and "dead" under the
other, and a gate that is green under dash is not proved under bash.

THE RULE. Validate the pid's SHAPE first, as ONE `case` on the whole string, so that removing the check is RED under any
sh. A digits-only test placed after `kill -0` would be masked by bash. cyrius's check.sh reaps a dead process's capture
file `audit_x[ca]_<pid>[.N]` this way since 6.7.16 (`_chk_reap_dead_capture_files`: a regular file the user owns, older
than `CYRIUS_CHECK_REAP_MINS`, its pid dead). Related: bash_pre_increment_set_e_zero_exit (a status that encodes data).
CHANGELOG [6.7.16] LF-4.

[[entries]]
name = "what_cyrius_lint_and_cyrius_lsp_accept_v6716"
description = "Since 6.7.16 cyrius-lsp checks a project file through its compile unit (a fragment's diagnostics are its entry's, read on that file; a file only an aarch64 or cx entry includes gets one Information note; files under `[lsp] skip` are never compiled), and drops CYRIUS_TARGET_* from its project children. `cycc_cx --syntax-only` is not cycc's: it accepts a reachable call to an undefined fn — use `cyrius lint`. And the lanes moved `cyrius lint`'s verdict on several shapes, each to the build's."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "cyrius lint", "--syntax-only", "cyrius-lsp", "lsp", "[lsp] skip", "compile unit", "fragment", "cycc_cx", "CYRIUS_TARGET", "cyrius.cyml", "diagnostics"]
---
    [lsp]
    skip = ["bootstrap/cybs.cyr", "src/main_aarch64_native.cyr"]    # cyrius's own: never compiled by the server

CYRIUS-LSP IN A PROJECT (6.7.16, TL-13, TL-15). Through 6.7.15 the open file was always the compile unit, so a file that
is an `include` of another read every name an earlier include declares as undefined (55 false errors on the compiler's
own parse_ctrl.cyr, a duplicate-fn warning for each fn of lib/string.cyr). Since 6.7.16:
  - a fragment's diagnostics are its ENTRY's, read on that file; one compile serves every fragment of a unit until a
    file it depends on changes on disk;
  - a file only an aarch64 or cx entry includes shows one Information note instead of diagnostics;
  - list the `.cyr` files that are not cycc input — generated text, assembly, a fork built only raw by another compiler
    — under `[lsp] skip` in cyrius.cyml. An older installed CLI warns `[lsp]` is not a known section: harmless;
  - the server drops an inherited CYRIUS_TARGET_* / CYRIUS_MACHO* from its project checks: set a target per unit through
    the include graph, not the editor's environment.
cyrius-lsp runs on macOS since 6.7.16 too (it published nothing there; platform_abi.cyml
the_aarch64_linux_install_has_the_toolchain_but_typescript_v6714).

cycc_cx's `--syntax-only` IS NOT THE NATIVE COMPILERS' (TL-10). Since 6.7.16 it accepts a sibling module's fn as the
natives do (it refused the file, listing the fn once per call), and it ALSO accepts a reachable call to an undefined fn
— it has no reachability data. It writes no `.cyx` under the flag. For the lint verdict use cycc's `--syntax-only`,
which is what `cyrius lint` runs.

`cyrius lint` VERDICTS THE LANES MOVED (each now the build's, or the sibling-module rule's):
  - accepted: `gp().n = 5;` for `fn gp(): *Foo` with Foo a sibling module's struct (ST-9); a sibling's generic called
    with explicit type arguments, `g<i64>(x)` (RV-13); a chain on a receiver whose struct the file cannot see, a named
    argument in it, a local of a sibling's struct type (GB-5, GB-5r, GB-7, GB-24). ⚠ A list naming a type the file does
    not know — a sibling's STRUCT, `g<Pt2>(x)` — is still a false "expected ';', got '>'";
  - refused, as the build refuses: `(s).a = 1;` and `Some(5).n = 1;` above its enum (SS-6, SS-2); a third type parameter
    (6.7.16's D8) and a trait member's `: *fn( .. )` return (D9);
  - silent, the compile refuses: a type-argument list longer than its generic declares (D5), `Name<..>` on a non-generic
    struct or a scalar in a by-value position (D7), `Color<..>` / `Option<..>` / a `Result<..>` argument (D88), a
    16-byte struct as a multi-value element (D90).
A file that lints clean can still fail its build: lint reads one file. CHANGELOG [6.7.16] TL-10, TL-13, TL-15, ST-9,
RV-13, GB-5, GB-7, GB-24, SS-2, SS-6, GS-5, GS-8.
